{
  "issue": 61,
  "kind": "bug",
  "understanding": "The previous work order's three edits were right, and QA says so: T-1 through T-7 all pass, and each of the five mutations turns the suite red at the corresponding assertion. What was wrong was the premise underneath its first criterion, and that premise is the whole reason this run exists.\n\nAC-1 claims the skip link is 'the first focusable element a keyboard reaches from a fresh page load', and its how_to_verify names ?state=error on /journal explicitly. The plan verified that claim as a DOCUMENT-ORDER property and built its lock accordingly — `skip.index < main.index`, two offsets in a rendered HTML string. Focus order is a different axis. A page can put the skip link first in the DOM and still hand focus to an element deep in the document before the user has pressed a key; the first Tab then continues from that element instead of from the top, and the bypass is dead. src/web/client.js:35-40 does exactly that on every error shell: it autofocuses `.panel-error [data-action=\"retry\"]` on load. So attempt 1 wrote a criterion against a property it never executed, and the lock it added is structurally incapable of seeing the failure — every guard in test/web/pages.test.js reads an HTML string, and this focus is set by script at runtime. I reproduced it in Chromium on the PR head (22204ea): on /journal?state=error, document.activeElement is already BUTTON[data-action=retry] before any key is pressed, and one Tab lands on the first journal Open button, never on A.skip-link; on /?state=error it lands on BODY after the ring cycles out of the document; on /journal with no ?state= it is BODY -> A.skip-link, which is why four of the five states pass. The same focus also runs on a REAL failure (?meta_error=quota), where body data-state is still 'ideal', so the only thing a screen reader is told is 'Command dashboard is showing the ideal preview state' — the focus steal was substituting for an announcement that does not exist.\n\nI considered amending AC-1 to exclude the error state, and rejected it. Amending it is only correct when the criterion contradicts itself or states a rule the code obeys, and neither is true here: docs/ui.md:114 independently requires 'logical tab order matching the region order', which a load-time focus into a panel below the nav violates, and docs/ui.md:65 requires the error state to OFFER retry, which it still does. The criterion is right and the code is wrong, so the code changes. AC-1 stays exactly as written, id and position intact.\n\nThe second bug is smaller and is the plan's own half-finished work. The plan restored `id=\"main\"` to mainOf and to the skip-link guard using \\b, a WORD boundary rather than whitespace — and '-' is a non-word character, so \\bid=\"main\" matches the tail of data-id=\"main\". I reproduced it: with `<main id=\"main\" tabindex=\"-1\">` changed to `<main data-id=\"main\" tabindex=\"-1\">`, the PR head's suite is 53 pass / 0 fail while the page in a browser serves a skip link whose href=\"#main\" resolves to nothing. The new pin covers <main> and <main id=\"other\"> but not the data-id shape, which is the one hole left in the family it was written to close. Every other attribute matcher in that helper has the same defect (data-tabindex, data-class, data-href), so the fix closes the family rather than the one instance QA happened to mutate.\n\nSo: the previous root_cause was not wrong about what it fixed, but it was incomplete in a way that mattered — it treated 'the skip link precedes main in the document' as the bypass guarantee when it is only half of it, and it did not notice that the other half is set by a line of JavaScript four issues old (#30) that no HTML-string assertion can reach.",
  "root_cause": "Two causes, one per bug, and only one of them is in the diff this ticket locked.\n\nBUG-1: the bypass guarantee was specified and verified as a document-order property when focus order is a different axis. src/web/client.js:35-40 autofocuses `.panel-error [data-action=\"retry\"]` unconditionally on load, so on every error shell activeElement is already deep in the document before the user has pressed anything, and the first Tab continues from there — the skip link is never the first stop. Nothing in the suite can see it: every guard in test/web/pages.test.js asserts against the rendered HTML string, and this is a runtime script side effect. The load-time focus was also doing a job it cannot do — it stands in for an announcement of the failure that does not exist, and on a region-level failure (?meta_error=) the page state is still 'ideal', so the live region names nothing that failed.\n\nBUG-2: both landmark guards ask for their attribute with \\b, which asserts a word boundary, not whitespace. '-' is a non-word character, so \\bid=\"main\" matches the tail of data-id=\"main\" and the same is true of \\btabindex / \\bclass / \\bhref in the same helper. A landmark carrying only data-id=\"main\" is not the skip link's target — href=\"#main\" resolves to nothing — and the suite is green under exactly that mutation.",
  "approach": "Two files. One behavioural change in the client script, one hardening pass over the guards, plus the tests that make both un-regressable. I ran every step of this before writing it; the reproduction and verification commands are in evidence[].\n\n**1. src/web/client.js — stop taking focus on load; announce the failure instead.** Replace the unconditional `failedRetry.focus()` block (lines 35-40) with a query for `.panel-error` and an `announce()` of the panel's own `<h2>` text plus ' Use Retry to try again.'. The heading is the panel's failure name, which docs/ui.md:47 and :65 already require the panel to state, so the announcement cannot drift from the page and no new copy is invented. Read the heading defensively (an empty or missing <h2> falls back to a generic sentence) so the block cannot throw on a shell that renders differently.\n\nThis keeps what the autofocus was FOR — a screen-reader user landing on a failed page is told what failed and that there is a retry — and drops what it was doing wrong. Removing focus on load is not a smaller change than scoping it: there is no in-page error transition in v1 to scope to. Every error panel is server-rendered, the retry is a `window.location.replace`, and the only moment the script runs is a full page load, which is exactly the moment a fresh-load focus order is being asserted. Verified in Chromium: on all four error shells plus /?meta_error=quota, activeElement is BODY before any key and A.skip-link after one Tab, and the live region reads e.g. 'Replay evaluation failed for scenario replay-tracking-outage. Use Retry to try again.' and, on the region-level failure, 'Meta provider sync failed. Use Retry to try again.' while body data-state is still 'ideal'. The retry click wiring, the 'Retrying…' announcement and the ?state= stripping are untouched and each is pinned by a test.\n\n**2. test/web/pages.test.js — require whitespace before the attribute, in all four matchers.** In `mainOf` and in every attribute clause of `skipLinkFocusesMain` (`id`, `tabindex`, `class`, `href`), change `\\b` to `\\s`. All four are matched against a capture that begins immediately after the tag name, so a real attribute is always preceded by whitespace and `data-*` never is. Do not narrow it to the id alone: that is the same one-line change four times, and leaving three siblings behind is how this comes back. Add one negative pin per attribute (`data-id`, `data-tabindex`, `data-class`, `data-href`) plus the data-id negative for `mainOf`, and rewrite the helper's comment to say why \\b is the wrong boundary. Verified: with the data-id mutation applied to src/web/pages.js, the PR head is 53 pass / 0 fail while the fixed suite goes to 51 pass / 2 fail — the heading-hierarchy test and the skip-link guard, the two QA said should both reject it.\n\n**3. Same file — the focus harness and its three tests.** Add an `errorShellHarness()` beside the existing `draftHarness()`. It records `focus()` calls rather than faking focus, because the claim under test is which element is focused before any key press. One detail is load-bearing and is how I caught a first attempt at this test being mutation-invisible: the panel stub must answer ANY `.panel-error` selector, including the descendant form a pre-fix client.js used (`'.panel-error [data-action=\"retry\"]'`), and carry its own `focus()` recorder. A stub that only answers `'.panel-error'` makes the test pass against the unfixed script, because the unfixed script never asks that question. With the agnostic stub, restoring the old client.js turns the new tests red (54 pass / 2 fail); with the fix they are green.\n\n**4. Same file — one hardening the PR's own risk section named.** QA recorded that the source ORDER of the `@media (forced-colors: active)` block is asserted by no test: both selectors are 1,1,0, so moving the carve-out above the bare `#main:focus { outline: none }` kills it, and today only an older test's 'first match wins' heuristic notices by accident. Add one assertion that the carve-out's `#main:focus` appears after the first bare `#main:focus` in src/web/styles.css. It is not a bug fix — no bug was filed — it converts a risk this diff shipped with into a lock, and it is two lines.\n\n**What this deliberately does not do.** No change to src/web/pages.js: the shipped markup already places the skip link before <main> and the landmark already carries id=\"main\". No change to the `outline: none` rule itself, to the forced-colors carve-out's declaration, or to the drawer focus management in client.js (opening a drawer moves focus in, closing returns it to the invoking row) — that is focus management on a deliberate user action, which is exactly what load-time focus is not. No criterion is rewritten.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "src/web/client.js",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Replace the block at lines 35-40 — the comment 'An error panel moves focus to its retry action…' together with `var failedRetry = document.querySelector('.panel-error [data-action=\"retry\"]'); if (failedRetry) { failedRetry.focus(); }` — with a `.panel-error` query that calls announce() instead of focus(). Announce the panel's own heading text plus ' Use Retry to try again.'; read the heading defensively so a shell without an <h2> falls back to a generic sentence rather than throwing. Rewrite the comment to state the reason the focus is gone: it put the keyboard entry point deep in the document so the first Tab after a fresh load started past the skip link (issue #61, AC-1), and the live region is the mechanism that reaches a screen reader without taking focus. Leave every other statement in the file exactly as it is — the `[data-action=\"retry\"]` click wiring and its 'Retrying…' announcement, the localStorage draft restore, and the drawer's focus-in / return-focus. The file must still contain zero `innerHTML` occurrences (test/web/drawer.test.js asserts it against the served asset).",
      "why": "BUG-1. This single line is why the first Tab does not reach the skip link on any of the five error shells, and it is the one behaviour in the product that can pre-empt the bypass."
    },
    {
      "path": "test/web/pages.test.js",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Four edits, all in this file. (a) In the `mainOf` constant and in every attribute clause of `skipLinkFocusesMain`, replace the `\\b` before `id`, `tabindex`, `class` and `href` with `\\s` — four occurrences, not one. The captured attribute string begins immediately after the tag name, so a real attribute is always preceded by whitespace and a `data-*` spelling never is. (b) Extend the pin block at the end of 'the skip link targets a focusable main landmark on every route and state' with four negatives: a landmark carrying `data-id=\"main\"`, a `data-tabindex=\"-1\"`, a `data-class=\"skip-link\"` and a `data-href=\"#main\"`; and extend the `mainOf` pin test with `<main data-id=\"main\"><h1>X</h1></main>` -> ''. (c) Add `errorShellHarness()` beside the existing `draftHarness()`, plus the three tests named in tests[]. The harness must record focus() calls rather than fake focus, and the panel stub must answer ANY selector containing '.panel-error' — including the descendant form '.panel-error [data-action=\"retry\"]' — and carry its own focus() recorder, or the test passes against the unfixed script. (d) Add one assertion that the `@media (forced-colors: active)` carve-out's `#main:focus` appears AFTER the first bare `#main:focus` in src/web/styles.css, so a reordering cannot silently kill a rule that wins only on source order. Keep every existing assertion in the file: the three pre-#61 negatives, the two order-tolerant positives, the brand/nav-before-the-link positive, the three null-document guards, the mainOf acceptances and its two existing rejections, both stylesheet-reading tests, and the whole 25-shell route x state loop.",
      "why": "BUG-2 is in both guards, and the new pins are the only thing that makes the data-id shape non-regressable. The focus harness is the first assertion in this file's history that can see a runtime focus side effect at all — every other guard here reads an HTML string, which is why this defect survived a review and a QA run."
    }
  ],
  "tests": [
    {
      "path": "test/web/pages.test.js",
      "action": "edit",
      "cases": [
        "the reproduction of QA's BUG-1: with a minimal error-shell DOM whose panel carries a focus() recorder, loading the shipped client.js focuses NOTHING — assert.deepEqual(focused, []) — on the error shell AND on a shell with no error panel at all. This is the case that was mutation-invisible on a first attempt: the panel stub must answer any '.panel-error' selector, descendant form included, or the pre-fix script never calls focus() in the harness and the test passes green against the defect. Verified red against the unfixed client.js (54 pass / 2 fail).",
        "the replacement contract, so the fix cannot later be 'simplified' by deleting the announce call and leaving a failure nobody is told about: on an error shell the live region's text after load names what failed (the panel's own h2) and points at the Retry action. Assert both halves.",
        "the behaviour the autofocus displaced, minus the focus steal: activating the wired retry still announces 'Retrying…' and still reloads the route with the ?state= preview override stripped (a /journal?state=error retry lands on '/journal').",
        "the reproduction of QA's BUG-2 in both guards: skipLinkFocusesMain must reject <main data-id=\"main\" tabindex=\"-1\">, <main id=\"main\" data-tabindex=\"-1\">, <a data-class=\"skip-link\" href=\"#main\"> and <a class=\"skip-link\" data-href=\"#main\">; and mainOf must reject <main data-id=\"main\"><h1>X</h1></main> while still accepting <main id=\"main\">, <main id=\"main\" tabindex=\"-1\">, <main tabindex=\"-1\" id=\"main\"> and a tolerated fourth attribute. Verified: with the data-id mutation applied to src/web/pages.js, the PR head is 53 pass / 0 fail and this suite is 51 pass / 2 fail, red at the heading-hierarchy test and the skip-link guard.",
        "the forced-colors carve-out is asserted to come AFTER the bare `#main:focus` rule, so the two rules at equal specificity keep the outcome the browser measured. Moving the carve-out above the bare rule must turn this test red.",
        "nothing existing may break: the 25-shell route x state loop, the three pre-#61 negatives, the brand/nav-before-the-link positive, the two attribute-order positives, the three null-document guards, both stylesheet-reading tests, the H1_HOOK pins, and every currency and drawer test in the suite. `npm run sdlc:verify` (node --test) reports 280 pass / 0 fail repo-wide — 277 on the PR head, plus the three added here."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "acceptance": [
    {
      "id": "AC-1",
      "check": "On every route and in every state, the skip link is the first focusable element a keyboard reaches from a fresh page load, and it precedes the main landmark in document order.",
      "how_to_verify": "Load http://localhost:3000/ , then each of /journal, /opportunities, /experiments, /approvals. Press Tab once from a fresh load with no prior interaction: the first element to receive focus is the skip link (it becomes visible at top-left on focus, per `.skip-link:focus`). Confirm it is a link to #main and that the main container it targets is the one holding the page's h1. Repeat once with ?state=error on /journal to cover a non-ideal shell. The order is also asserted at the DOM level: `document.querySelector('.skip-link').compareDocumentPosition(document.getElementById('main')) & Node.DOCUMENT_POSITION_FOLLOWING` must be truthy on every route and state.",
      "verify": "browser"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-2",
      "check": "Activating the skip link moves keyboard focus to the main landmark, and that landmark paints a focus indicator when the browser is in forced-colors mode.",
      "how_to_verify": "Tab to the skip link, press Enter, and confirm focus lands inside the main landmark (the h1 'Command dashboard' on /) rather than back on <body> — this is the #52 behaviour and must not regress. For the forced-colors half, the stylesheet is not observable in a default browser session, so it is verified by the named test: 'the #main focus suppression is restored inside forced-colors so the landmark is not the one focusable thing with no indicator' in test/web/pages.test.js, which reads src/web/styles.css, asserts the `@media (forced-colors: active)` block exists and declares `outline: 2px solid CanvasText`, and goes red if the block is deleted. The existing test 'the #main focus suppression still carries the declaration the skip target depends on' must still pass, proving the default-mode suppression was not simply removed.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-3",
      "check": "The heading-hierarchy assertion remains tied to the main landmark that carries id=\"main\" — the skip link's target — and not to some other <main> element.",
      "how_to_verify": "No browser can see which regex the test uses, so this is the named test: 'the main matcher still requires id=\"main\" on the landmark it captures' in test/web/pages.test.js. It must assert the matcher matches `<main id=\"main\">`, `<main id=\"main\" tabindex=\"-1\">` and `<main tabindex=\"-1\" id=\"main\">` and rejects both `<main>` and `<main id=\"other\">`. CI runs it under `npm run sdlc:verify`; the two rejections are the specific coverage #52 dropped.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-4",
      "check": "The full suite stays green: `npm run sdlc:verify` (`npm ci --no-audit --no-fund && node --test`) reports 0 failures across every test file, not only the one edited here.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run `npm run sdlc:verify` and read the `# fail 0` line. test/web/pages.test.js reports 53 passing on the PR head and 56 after this change; the count may rise by the pins added here but no test anywhere may fail.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-5",
      "check": "On a fresh page load, nothing on the page holds keyboard focus before the user has pressed a key, on any route and in any state including the error state, so the first Tab reaches the skip link rather than continuing from wherever the page put focus.",
      "how_to_verify": "This is QA BUG-1, reproduced on every shell, so check all of them rather than one. In a fresh window open http://localhost:3000/journal?state=error and read `document.activeElement` with NO key pressed: it must be <body>, not the Retry button. Press Tab exactly once: it must be A.skip-link[href=\"#main\"]. Repeat the identical check on /?state=error, /opportunities?state=error, /experiments?state=error, /approvals?state=error and on /journal with no override as the control. Also check a real failure rather than a preview shell: /?meta_error=quota, where the page still renders an error panel and body data-state stays 'ideal' — the same expectation holds there, which is the case the load-time focus was silently standing in for.",
      "verify": "browser"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-6",
      "check": "A landmark or link whose required attribute appears only inside a data-* attribute is not treated as carrying it: a <main data-id=\"main\"> with no id is not the skip link's target, and neither is a data-tabindex, a data-class or a data-href.",
      "how_to_verify": "No browser can see which regex a test uses, so this is the named tests: the four new negatives in 'the skip link targets a focusable main landmark on every route and state' and the data-id rejection in 'the main matcher still requires id=\"main\" on the landmark it captures', both in test/web/pages.test.js. They are asserted against the REAL helpers, not copies. The mutation that proves they bite: change `<main id=\"main\" tabindex=\"-1\">` to `<main data-id=\"main\" tabindex=\"-1\">` in a scratch copy of src/web/pages.js and run `node --test test/web/pages.test.js` — it must go red at the heading-hierarchy test and at the skip-link guard. It is 53 pass / 0 fail on the PR head under that same mutation, which is QA BUG-2.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-7",
      "check": "A page that renders an error panel tells an assistive technology what failed, and that the retry action is available, without taking focus away from the top of the document.",
      "how_to_verify": "On any error shell, read `document.getElementById('live-region').textContent` after load: it must name the failure the panel itself names (on /journal?state=error, 'Replay evaluation failed for scenario replay-tracking-outage'; on /, 'Provider sync failed'; on /?meta_error=quota, 'Meta provider sync failed', even though body data-state there is still 'ideal') and point at Retry. The retry button must still be reachable and still work: activate it and confirm the page reloads the route with the ?state= preview override stripped. This is the mechanism that replaced the load-time focus, so it is locked as a contract of its own — without it, deleting the focus would have left a failed page that says nothing.",
      "verify": "browser"
    }
  ],
  "qa_script": [
    "npm run sdlc:verify — confirm 0 failures repo-wide, not just in test/web/pages.test.js. Expected: 280 pass / 0 fail (277 on the PR head, plus three new tests).",
    "Boot the app (PORT=3000 npm run sdlc:serve) and load each of /, /journal, /opportunities, /experiments, /approvals.",
    "THE CHECK THAT FAILED LAST RUN — do it first. On every error shell (/journal?state=error, /?state=error, /opportunities?state=error, /experiments?state=error, /approvals?state=error), open a FRESH window, read document.activeElement with no key pressed, and press Tab exactly once. Expect <body> then A.skip-link[href=\"#main\"], on all five. The pre-fix behaviour was BUTTON[data-action=retry] then a journal Open button or <body>.",
    "Same two reads on /?meta_error=quota — a real failure where the page state stays 'ideal', so it is the case the old focus was masking.",
    "On each of the five routes, press Tab once from a fresh load and confirm the FIRST focus stop is the visible skip link, not the brand or the nav.",
    "On each route, confirm the skip link precedes the main landmark in document order (compareDocumentPosition), including under ?state=loading, ?state=partial and ?state=error.",
    "Activate the skip link with the keyboard and confirm focus lands inside the main landmark on the h1, not on <body> — the #52 behaviour this change must not regress.",
    "Confirm the focused main landmark still paints NO accent ring in the default colour mode, and a visible 2px one under forced-colors emulation if the browser session allows it (the deterministic proof is the named test in AC-2).",
    "On an error shell, read #live-region after load and confirm it names the failure and points at Retry; then activate Retry and confirm the page reloads the route with ?state= stripped.",
    "Run the 'every route renders exactly one h1 that opens the hierarchy, in every state' test and confirm it still passes over all 25 shells with the tightened matcher.",
    "Confirm no page renders a second <main> landmark, which the tightened matcher would now distinguish."
  ],
  "risks": [
    "This removes a behaviour a human chose on purpose. The autofocus dates to #30, not to this ticket, and .sdlc/memory/qa/selectors.md:21 still records it as a QA fact ('focused automatically on error pages, so keyboard assertions start there'). That file is a reserved path and this change cannot correct it, so the line is now wrong and the Librarian should update it. Nothing in the suite depended on the focus — nothing tested it before, which is why it survived a review and two QA runs.",
    "A user who was relying on the focus jump now has to Tab from the top of the page to reach Retry. That is the trade AC-1 makes and docs/ui.md:114 backs, but it is a real change in behaviour and the implementer should say so in the PR body rather than presenting it as a pure test fix.",
    "The announcement text is asserted in a test, so editing the copy turns that test red. The copy is deliberately the panel's own <h2> — the failure name docs/ui.md:47 and :65 already require the panel to state — so it cannot drift from the page, but a test failure on 'an error shell is announced through the live region' means the copy changed, not that the harness broke.",
    "errorShellHarness stubs document. If client.js grows a code path that reaches for something the stub lacks (a future second dialog, say), the harness has to grow with it, and that failure reads as a harness gap rather than a product regression. Keep the stub's answer for '.panel-error' selector-agnostic so it cannot be made to pass by an implementation asking a different question of it.",
    "The forced-colors source-order assertion is an ordering pin. A deliberate stylesheet reorganisation will trip it, and the right response then is to re-measure the outline in a browser and re-justify, not to delete the assertion.",
    "No forbidden path is touched: src/web/client.js and test/web/pages.test.js are both outside reserved paths, and the planner's own never-touch list is untouched. The change adds no host, request or dependency, sends no data anywhere, and adds no global. env.api_allowlist is therefore not a constraint and QA will not be blocked on it."
  ],
  "out_of_scope": [
    "Editing .sdlc/memory/qa/selectors.md, whose line 21 documents the autofocus this change removes. It is a reserved path (the Librarian's); the correction belongs there, not in this ticket.",
    "Amending AC-1, or any other acceptance criterion, to carve out the error state. The criterion states a rule docs/ui.md:114 independently requires and the code does not obey; a criterion rewritten to match a defect is the one artefact that makes every future QA run certify it.",
    "src/web/pages.js. The shipped markup already places the skip link before <main> (pages.js:68 vs 77) and the landmark already carries id=\"main\" and tabindex=\"-1\". Both bugs are guards and a script, not the markup.",
    "The drawer's focus management in client.js. Opening a journal row moves focus into the dialog and closing returns it to the invoking row; that is focus management on a deliberate user action and is exactly what load-time focus is not. It stays.",
    "The '#main focus suppression still carries the declaration…' test and the `outline: none` rule it guards. The ring-around-1100px-of-main problem the rule solves is real and the forced-colors carve-out stays additive.",
    "Pinning that every shell renders exactly ONE <main> landmark. QA recorded this as a latent gap in skipLinkFocusesMain (it reads the first <main>, whatever its id) and confirmed T-2 shows exactly one on all 25 shells today. A single-landmark assertion over the matrix is a reasonable separate hardening; it is not a bug this run found, and folding it in would widen the diff past the two defects.",
    "Any other autofocus or focus call anywhere in src/web. `grep -rn '\\.focus(' src/` finds three: this one, the drawer's two. Only this one runs on load."
  ],
  "alternatives_considered": [
    {
      "approach": "Amend AC-1 so it covers the ideal, empty, loading and partial states and explicitly excludes the error state, on the argument that moving focus to the failing panel is a deliberate product decision and the criterion is what overreached.",
      "rejected_because": "This is the criterion rewrite that is only correct when the criterion is internally contradictory or the code obeys the rule it states, and neither holds. The criterion says the skip link is the first element a keyboard reaches from a fresh load; docs/ui.md:114 independently requires a tab order matching the region order; and the load-time focus announces nothing — on ?meta_error=quota the live region still says 'ideal' while a panel has failed, so the focus steal was standing in for a missing announcement rather than serving a purpose the criterion denies. QA's own note calls this 'a maintainer call', but the decision it defers is a product judgement about a11y, not a contradiction in the text, and this pipeline has exactly one stage that may correct a criterion — this one — and it may only do so to make the criterion say what the plan already meant. The plan meant the bypass must work. So the code changes instead."
    },
    {
      "approach": "Keep the autofocus but scope it so it cannot pre-empt the first Tab — for example, focus the retry only when the user has already interacted, or only when the error arrives in-page rather than on load.",
      "rejected_because": "There is no in-page error transition to scope to. Every error panel is server-rendered and the retry is a `window.location.replace`, so the script's only load-time moment IS a full page load, which is the moment the focus order is being asserted. A 'has the user interacted yet' guard is a heuristic that would need its own test and would still be a focus steal on the second error of a session. Verified: with the focus removed, all five error shells plus ?meta_error=quota give BODY -> A.skip-link in Chromium, and the failure is announced instead."
    },
    {
      "approach": "Fix only the id matcher (\\bid=\"main\" -> \\sid=\"main\") and leave \\btabindex, \\bclass and \\bhref as they are, since QA only mutated the id.",
      "rejected_because": "It is the same one-line change four times, and QA mutated the one that happened to be reachable. I checked the siblings: a landmark with data-tabindex=\"-1\" satisfies \\btabindex=\"-1\" and is equally not focusable, and a link with data-class=\"skip-link\" or data-href=\"#main\" satisfies the other two the same way. Leaving three behind is how this comes back on the next mutation, and the cost of closing them is three negative pins."
    },
